nano analyze_memory_performance.sh
#!/bin/bash
# Memory Performance Analysis Script

DATA_FILE="/var/log/sa/sa$(date +%d)"
REPORT_FILE="memory_analysis_report_$(date +%Y%m%d).txt"

echo "Memory Performance Analysis Report" > $REPORT_FILE
echo "Generated on: $(date)" >> $REPORT_FILE
echo "===================================" >> $REPORT_FILE

echo -e "\n1. Memory Utilization Overview:" >> $REPORT_FILE
sar -r -f $DATA_FILE | grep -E "(Average|kbmemfree|kbmemused|%memused)" | tail -5 >> $REPORT_FILE

echo -e "\n2. High Memory Usage Periods (>80%):" >> $REPORT_FILE
sar -r -f $DATA_FILE | awk '$4 != "%memused" && NF>8 && $4 > 80 {print $1, $2, "Memory Used:", $4"%", "Available:", $6"KB"}' >> $REPORT_FILE

echo -e "\n3. Memory Efficiency Summary:" >> $REPORT_FILE
sar -r -f $DATA_FILE | awk '
BEGIN {samples=0; total_used=0; total_free=0}
$4 != "%memused" && NF > 8 {
    samples++;
    total_used += $4;
    total_free += $2;
}
END {
    if(samples > 0) {
        avg_used = total_used/samples;
        avg_free = total_free/samples;
        print "Average Memory Usage:", avg_used"%";
        print "Average Free Memory:", avg_free"KB";
    }
}' >> $REPORT_FILE

echo "Memory analysis complete. Report saved to: $REPORT_FILE"
cat $REPORT_FILE
chmod +x analyze_memory_performance.sh
./analyze_memory_performance.sh
Task 2 Deliverable: Two report files (cpu_analysis_report_*.txt and memory_analysis_report_*.txt) each show a computed "Average" percentage and at least one flagged high-usage period corresponding to the stress-test windows.

Task 3: Analyze Disk I/O and Generate a Consolidated Report
Subtask 3.1: Generate Disk I/O Load and Collect Data
nano disk_stress_test.sh
#!/bin/bash
# Disk I/O Stress Test Script

TEST_DIR="/tmp/disk_test"
mkdir -p $TEST_DIR

echo "Starting disk I/O stress test..."

dd if=/dev/zero of=${TEST_DIR}/seq_write_test bs=1M count=200 conv=fsync
dd if=/dev/urandom of=${TEST_DIR}/random_test bs=4k count=5000 oflag=direct
dd if=${TEST_DIR}/random_test of=/dev/null bs=4k iflag=direct

rm -rf $TEST_DIR
echo "Disk stress test completed"
chmod +x disk_stress_test.sh
./disk_stress_test.sh &
Collect disk data while the stress runs:

sar -d 5 20
sar -b 5 10
Deliverable: sar -d 5 20 displays non-zero tps and %util values for the device backing /tmp during the test, confirming I/O activity is captured.

Subtask 3.2: Analyze Disk Data and Build the Master Report
nano analyze_disk_performance.sh
#!/bin/bash
# Disk Performance Analysis Script

DATA_FILE="/var/log/sa/sa$(date +%d)"
REPORT_FILE="disk_analysis_report_$(date +%Y%m%d).txt"

echo "Disk Performance Analysis Report" > $REPORT_FILE
echo "Generated on: $(date)" >> $REPORT_FILE
echo "==================================" >> $REPORT_FILE

echo -e "\n1. Disk Utilization Overview:" >> $REPORT_FILE
sar -d -f $DATA_FILE | grep -E "(Average|DEV|tps|%util)" | tail -10 >> $REPORT_FILE

echo -e "\n2. High Disk Utilization Periods (>70%):" >> $REPORT_FILE
sar -d -f $DATA_FILE | awk '$NF != "%util" && NF>9 && $NF > 70 {print $1, $2, "Device:", $3, "Utilization:", $NF"%", "TPS:", $4}' >> $REPORT_FILE

echo -e "\n3. Disk Performance Summary:" >> $REPORT_FILE
sar -d -f $DATA_FILE | awk '
BEGIN {samples=0; total_tps=0; total_util=0}
$4 != "tps" && NF > 9 {
    samples++;
    total_tps += $4;
    total_util += $NF;
}
END {
    if(samples > 0) {
        avg_tps = total_tps/samples;
        avg_util = total_util/samples;
        print "Average TPS:", avg_tps;
        print "Average Disk Utilization:", avg_util"%";
    }
}' >> $REPORT_FILE

echo "Disk analysis complete. Report saved to: $REPORT_FILE"
cat $REPORT_FILE
chmod +x analyze_disk_performance.sh
./analyze_disk_performance.sh
Now build a consolidated master report combining CPU, memory, and disk findings:

nano master_performance_analysis.sh
#!/bin/bash
# Master Performance Analysis Script

DATA_FILE="/var/log/sa/sa$(date +%d)"
REPORT_DIR="/tmp/performance_reports"
TIMESTAMP=$(date +%Y%m%d_%H%M%S)
MASTER_REPORT="${REPORT_DIR}/master_performance_report_${TIMESTAMP}.txt"

mkdir -p $REPORT_DIR

{
echo "System Performance Analysis Report"
echo "Generated: $(date)"
echo "Hostname: $(hostname)"
echo "Kernel: $(uname -r)"
echo "Uptime: $(uptime)"
echo "===================================="

echo ""
echo "CPU Performance Summary"
sar -u -f $DATA_FILE | awk '
$3 != "%user" && NF > 6 {
    samples++; total_idle+=$8; total_user+=$3; total_system+=$5;
    util = 100-$8;
    if(util > max_util) max_util = util;
}
END {
    if(samples>0) {
        avg_util = 100 - (total_idle/samples);
        print "Average CPU Utilization: " avg_util "%";
        print "Peak CPU Utilization: " max_util "%";
        if (avg_util < 50) print "Status: GOOD";
        else if (avg_util < 80) print "Status: MODERATE";
        else print "Status: HIGH - Investigation needed";
    }
}'

echo ""
echo "Memory Performance Summary"
sar -r -f $DATA_FILE | awk '
$4 != "%memused" && NF > 8 {
    samples++; total_used+=$4;
    if($4 > max_used) max_used=$4;
}
END {
    if(samples>0) {
        avg_used = total_used/samples;
        print "Average Memory Usage: " avg_used "%";
        print "Peak Memory Usage: " max_used "%";
        if (avg_used < 70) print "Status: GOOD";
        else if (avg_used < 85) print "Status: MODERATE";
        else print "Status: HIGH - Investigation needed";
    }
}'

echo ""
echo "Disk Performance Summary"
sar -d -f $DATA_FILE | awk '
$4 != "tps" && NF > 9 {
    samples++; total_util+=$NF;
    if($NF > max_util) max_util=$NF;
}
END {
    if(samples>0) {
        avg_util = total_util/samples;
        print "Average Disk Utilization: " avg_util "%";
        print "Peak Disk Utilization: " max_util "%";
        if (avg_util < 50) print "Status: GOOD";
        else if (avg_util < 80) print "Status: MODERATE";
        else print "Status: HIGH - Investigation needed";
    }
}'

echo ""
echo "Performance Optimization Recommendations"
echo "1. CPU: monitor top offenders with top/htop; consider affinity or scaling."
echo "2. Memory: review memory-intensive processes; watch for swap growth."
echo "3. Disk: consider faster storage or I/O scheduler tuning for high-util devices."
echo "4. Establish baselines and enable ongoing automated monitoring (see Subtask 3.3)."
} > $MASTER_REPORT

echo "Master performance report generated: $MASTER_REPORT"
cat $MASTER_REPORT
chmod +x master_performance_analysis.sh
./master_performance_analysis.sh
Deliverable: /tmp/performance_reports/master_performance_report_*.txt exists and contains three status lines (CPU, Memory, Disk), each reading GOOD, MODERATE, or HIGH.

Subtask 3.3: Create Automated Monitoring Setup
To keep performance visibility ongoing beyond this lab session, create a monitoring script that runs the master analysis on a schedule and retains a rolling history of reports.

sudo nano /usr/local/bin/automated_performance_monitor.sh
Add the following content:

#!/bin/bash
# Automated Performance Monitoring Script
# Runs the master performance analysis and prunes old reports.

DATA_FILE="/var/log/sa/sa$(date +%d)"
REPORT_DIR="/var/log/performance_reports"
TIMESTAMP=$(date +%Y%m%d_%H%M%S)
REPORT_FILE="${REPORT_DIR}/perf_report_${TIMESTAMP}.txt"
RETENTION_DAYS=14
ALERT_LOG="${REPORT_DIR}/alerts.log"

mkdir -p "$REPORT_DIR"

{
    echo "Automated Performance Report - $(date)"
    echo "========================================"

    echo ""
    echo "CPU Summary:"
    sar -u -f "$DATA_FILE" | awk '
    $3 != "%user" && NF > 6 {
        samples++; total_idle+=$8;
    }
    END {
        if (samples > 0) {
            avg_util = 100 - (total_idle/samples);
            printf "Average CPU Utilization: %.2f%%\n", avg_util;
            if (avg_util > 85) print "ALERT: CPU utilization exceeds 85%";
        }
    }'

    echo ""
    echo "Memory Summary:"
    sar -r -f "$DATA_FILE" | awk '
    $4 != "%memused" && NF > 8 {
        samples++; total_used+=$4;
    }
    END {
        if (samples > 0) {
            avg_used = total_used/samples;
            printf "Average Memory Usage: %.2f%%\n", avg_used;
            if (avg_used > 90) print "ALERT: Memory usage exceeds 90%";
        }
    }'

    echo ""
    echo "Disk Summary:"
    sar -d -f "$DATA_FILE" | awk '
    $4 != "tps" && NF > 9 {
        samples++; total_util+=$NF;
    }
    END {
        if (samples > 0) {
            avg_util = total_util/samples;
            printf "Average Disk Utilization: %.2f%%\n", avg_util;
            if (avg_util > 80) print "ALERT: Disk utilization exceeds 80%";
        }
    }'
} > "$REPORT_FILE"

# Collect any ALERT lines into a persistent alert log with a timestamp
grep "ALERT" "$REPORT_FILE" | while read -r line; do
    echo "$(date): $line" >> "$ALERT_LOG"
done

# Remove reports older than the retention period
find "$REPORT_DIR" -name "perf_report_*.txt" -mtime +$RETENTION_DAYS -delete

echo "Report written to $REPORT_FILE"
Make the script executable:

sudo chmod +x /usr/local/bin/automated_performance_monitor.sh
Test it manually before scheduling:

sudo /usr/local/bin/automated_performance_monitor.sh
ls -la /var/log/performance_reports/
Option A: Schedule with cron (simplest, works on both RHEL/CentOS and Ubuntu):

sudo crontab -e
Add the following line to run the monitor every 30 minutes:

*/30 * * * * /usr/local/bin/automated_performance_monitor.sh
Option B: Schedule with a systemd service and timer (preferred for systems that already manage services via systemd):

sudo nano /etc/systemd/system/perf-monitor.service
[Unit]
Description=Automated Performance Monitoring Run

[Service]
Type=oneshot
ExecStart=/usr/local/bin/automated_performance_monitor.sh
sudo nano /etc/systemd/system/perf-monitor.timer
[Unit]
Description=Run Automated Performance Monitor every 30 minutes

[Timer]
OnBootSec=5min
OnUnitActiveSec=30min
Unit=perf-monitor.service

[Install]
WantedBy=timers.target
Enable and start the timer:

sudo systemctl daemon-reload
sudo systemctl enable --now perf-monitor.timer

# Verify the timer is scheduled
systemctl list-timers | grep perf-monitor
Task 3 Deliverable: Either crontab -l (run as the scheduling user) shows the */30 * * * * entry, or systemctl list-timers lists perf-monitor.timer with a valid next-run time. After waiting for at least one scheduled run, /var/log/performance_reports/ contains a new perf_report_*.txt file, and if any threshold was exceeded, /var/log/performance_reports/alerts.log contains a corresponding timestamped ALERT line.

Expected Outcomes
A working sysstat/sar installation collecting historical data at 2-minute intervals, verifiable via files in /var/log/sa/
Three analysis reports (CPU, memory, disk) plus one consolidated master report, each containing computed averages, peaks, and a GOOD/MODERATE/HIGH status classification
A scheduled automated monitoring job (cron or systemd timer) that regularly produces new performance reports and logs threshold alerts without manual intervention
Troubleshooting
If sar -f /var/log/sa/saXX returns "Cannot open" or produces no output, investigate whether the sysstat service is actually running, whether the file exists for today's date, and whether your user has permission to read files under /var/log/sa/. What does systemctl status sysstat tell you, and does the timestamp of the data file match when you expect data collection to have occurred?
If your analysis scripts produce empty or zero-value sections (for example, "Average CPU Utilization: 0%" with no peak periods flagged), check whether the awk field numbers used in the script actually match the columns in your system's sar output, since column positions can shift between sysstat versions or between CPU/disk/memory report types. Try running the raw sar command alone and counting the columns before assuming the stress test failed to generate load.
Conclusion
In this lab, students installed and configured sysstat's sar utility, verified continuous historical data collection, and built a series of bash scripts to analyze CPU, memory, and disk performance from that historical data. Students generated controlled CPU, memory, and disk workloads, correlated the resulting sar metrics with each workload phase, and produced a consolidated master performance report with clear status indicators. Finally, students implemented an ongoing automated monitoring solution, scheduled through either cron or a systemd timer, that periodically generates reports and logs threshold-based alerts. With this foundation, students are prepared to extend the monitoring scripts with alerting integrations such as email or messaging webhooks, tune collection intervals and retention policies for production environments, and use the historical sar archive to support long-term capacity planning decisions.
